1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,190
>> Right now, you're moving around the Sun at about 30 km/s.

2
00:00:06,270 --> 00:00:17,080
>> Not steadily, though. In early January, Earth moves about 1 km/s faster than in early July.

3
00:00:17,160 --> 00:00:21,450
>> Faster in January? That's the middle of winter in the north.

4
00:00:21,530 --> 00:00:30,770
>> The rule behind it was found more than 400 years ago, in old records of the planet Mars.

5
00:00:30,850 --> 00:00:37,490
>> And it started with an error of eight arcminutes. That's about a quarter of the width of the Moon.

6
00:00:37,570 --> 00:00:41,960
>> A quarter of the Moon. That's all it took?

7
00:00:42,040 --> 00:00:49,560
>> That's all. This is the story of Kepler's second law: equal areas in equal times.

8
00:00:54,360 --> 00:01:03,950
>> In the late 1500s, the best measurements of the sky came from a Danish nobleman, Tycho Brahe.

9
00:01:04,030 --> 00:01:12,170
>> From 1576 he worked on the island of Hven, at Uraniborg, an observatory funded by the king.

10
00:01:12,250 --> 00:01:15,820
>> And this was before telescopes, right?

11
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:21,970
>> Right, no telescopes yet. Tycho measured by eye, with large quadrants and sextants.

12
00:01:22,050 --> 00:01:29,740
>> And his positions came close to one arcminute. That's a sixtieth of a degree.

13
00:01:29,820 --> 00:01:32,560
>> That sounds tiny. How tiny?

14
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:43,050
>> The full Moon is about 31 arcminutes across. So, about a thirtieth of the Moon's width.

15
00:01:43,130 --> 00:01:50,600
>> And he kept at it year after year, recording where the planets were. Mars was among them.

16
00:01:51,100 --> 00:02:00,290
>> In 1597 he left Denmark, and soon he was working for the emperor, in Prague.

17
00:02:00,370 --> 00:02:07,640
>> And in February 1600, a 28-year-old mathematician joined him: Johannes Kepler.

18
00:02:07,720 --> 00:02:15,360
>> The most accurate observer of his time, and a mathematician to make sense of it.

19
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:22,010
>> Except Tycho guarded his data closely. He gave Kepler one planet to work on: Mars.

20
00:02:22,090 --> 00:02:35,580
>> Then in October 1601, Tycho died after a short illness. Two days later, Kepler took over as imperial mathematician.

21
00:02:35,660 --> 00:02:44,760
>> Tycho's heirs owned the observations, which would delay Kepler's book for years. But Kepler had the Mars data.

22
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:56,300
Now, astronomers had long modelled the planets with circles. Even Copernicus, who put the Sun near the middle, used circles.

23
00:02:56,380 --> 00:03:00,670
>> Circles riding on circles, in his case.

24
00:03:00,750 --> 00:03:10,440
So Kepler started with a circle too, with the Sun off-centre, and a point from which the motion looks steady.

25
00:03:10,520 --> 00:03:12,830
>> That point is called the equant.

26
00:03:12,910 --> 00:03:19,000
Fitting all that to Tycho's numbers meant one long calculation, over and over again.

27
00:03:19,080 --> 00:03:29,220
>> He complained about it in print: "Take pity on me, who had to go through with at least seventy repetitions of it."

28
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:31,890
>> Seventy times. By hand.

29
00:03:31,970 --> 00:03:40,610
>> And it nearly worked. The model matched Tycho's positions of Mars to within two arcminutes.

30
00:03:40,690 --> 00:03:45,680
>> But at other points in the orbit, it missed by up to eight.

31
00:03:45,760 --> 00:03:49,800
>> Couldn't he just call eight arcminutes close enough?

32
00:03:49,880 --> 00:03:55,770
>> Not with Tycho's data. It was far too accurate for an error that size to be ignored.

33
00:03:55,850 --> 00:04:07,490
>> Kepler wrote: "These eight minutes alone will lead us along a path to the reform of the whole of Astronomy."

34
00:04:07,570 --> 00:04:11,040
>> That's quite a promise to make about eight arcminutes.

35
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:21,020
>> Something in his model was wrong: either the circle, or the way the planet moved along it.

36
00:04:23,620 --> 00:04:34,640
Kepler wanted a physical cause. He imagined a force from the Sun, pushing the planets round, weaker with distance.

37
00:04:34,720 --> 00:04:41,560
>> So, he reasoned, a planet's speed falls as its distance grows. Twice as far, half as fast.

38
00:04:41,640 --> 00:04:45,030
>> Simple enough. So where's the catch?

39
00:04:45,110 --> 00:04:51,350
>> To find where the planet would be, he'd have to add up its distance from the Sun all along the way.

40
00:04:51,430 --> 00:04:55,800
>> At every point of the orbit? By hand?

41
00:04:55,880 --> 00:05:03,900
>> Exactly. So by late 1602 he'd found a shortcut: measure the area swept by the line from the Sun.

42
00:05:03,980 --> 00:05:15,820
>> Picture each day's motion as a thin triangle: its height is the distance from the Sun, its base the distance travelled.

43
00:05:15,900 --> 00:05:21,360
>> Far away, the triangle is tall but narrow. Close in, it's short but wide.

44
00:05:21,440 --> 00:05:30,540
>> And if speed falls as distance grows, every day's triangle has the same area.

45
00:05:30,640 --> 00:05:38,330
>> Equal areas in equal times. And here's the odd part: Kepler found this, the second law, before the first.

46
00:05:38,410 --> 00:05:42,820
>> So the numbering isn't the order he found them in.

47
00:05:42,900 --> 00:05:51,740
>> No. And his speed rule only works for orbits that are nearly circles. The area rule works for every orbit.

48
00:05:54,340 --> 00:05:59,030
Now he needed the shape. He tried egg-shaped ovals, again and again.

49
00:05:59,110 --> 00:06:00,670
>> How many?

50
00:06:00,750 --> 00:06:07,210
>> About forty failed attempts. Then, in late 1604, he tried an ellipse.

51
00:06:07,290 --> 00:06:15,280
>> Which he'd dismissed as too simple: surely earlier astronomers wouldn't have missed it.

52
00:06:15,360 --> 00:06:23,150
>> But it fitted. He'd even had the formula earlier, without seeing it. He wrote: "Ah, what a foolish bird I have been!"

53
00:06:23,230 --> 00:06:27,390
>> It's nice that he left that line in the book.

54
00:06:27,470 --> 00:06:39,110
So, the ellipse. It has two special points, its foci. The Sun sits at one. The other is empty.

55
00:06:39,190 --> 00:06:45,780
>> And that's Kepler's first law: a planet's orbit is an ellipse, with the Sun at one focus.

56
00:06:45,860 --> 00:06:55,450
>> Mars's ellipse is barely squashed, though: it's less than half a percent narrower than it is long.

57
00:06:55,530 --> 00:06:59,670
>> On this drawing, you can't even tell it from the dashed circle.

58
00:06:59,750 --> 00:07:08,120
>> But the Sun is well off-centre: more than 9% of the way from the middle to the end.

59
00:07:08,200 --> 00:07:15,160
>> Of the planets, only Mercury's orbit is more stretched. Kepler was lucky that his planet was Mars.

60
00:07:15,240 --> 00:07:17,780
>> Lucky, and very patient.

61
00:07:17,860 --> 00:07:27,780
In 1609 he published it all in a book: Astronomia nova, the New Astronomy.

62
00:07:30,380 --> 00:07:36,340
>> So where is a planet on a given day? Kepler turned the area rule into an equation.

63
00:07:36,420 --> 00:07:44,860
>> Time gives an angle, M, that ticks round steadily, like the hand of a clock.

64
00:07:44,940 --> 00:07:52,810
>> The planet's real place is set by another angle, E. And the little e says how stretched the ellipse is.

65
00:07:52,890 --> 00:08:03,180
>> Put together, that's Kepler's equation: M equals E, minus e times the sine of E.

66
00:08:03,260 --> 00:08:06,830
>> And here's the catch: you know M, and you want E.

67
00:08:06,910 --> 00:08:18,100
>> No algebra can solve it for E. So in 1621, Kepler suggested guessing, then improving the guess.

68
00:08:18,180 --> 00:08:27,120
>> For Mars, start with E equal to M, put each answer back in, and within four rounds the digits stop changing.

69
00:08:27,200 --> 00:08:33,990
>> So you never solve it exactly. You just get closer and closer.

70
00:08:34,070 --> 00:08:41,410
>> Right. This animation does that for every frame. Here's a stretched orbit, so the effect is easy to see.

71
00:08:41,490 --> 00:08:52,310
>> Split one trip around into twelve equal stretches of time, and shade the area each one sweeps.

72
00:08:53,760 --> 00:08:58,820
>> Near the Sun, the wedges are short and fat. Far away, they're long and thin.

73
00:08:58,900 --> 00:09:01,240
>> They look nothing alike.

74
00:09:01,320 --> 00:09:07,940
>> But measure them, and each one is a twelfth of the ellipse: 8.3%.

75
00:09:08,020 --> 00:09:13,790
>> While the distance travelled in each stretch is wildly different.

76
00:09:13,870 --> 00:09:23,170
>> On this orbit, the planet moves four times faster at its closest point, perihelion, than at aphelion, its farthest.

77
00:09:26,570 --> 00:09:32,240
>> Earth's orbit is much rounder. Does the rule still hold?

78
00:09:32,320 --> 00:09:34,660
>> It does, just more gently.

79
00:09:34,740 --> 00:09:43,350
Earth passes perihelion around 3 January, 147.1 million km from the Sun

80
00:09:43,430 --> 00:09:52,300
>> and aphelion around 4 July, at 152.1 million km.

81
00:09:52,380 --> 00:10:02,470
>> So it's fastest in January, about 30.3 km/s, and slowest in July, about 29.3.

82
00:10:02,550 --> 00:10:05,940
>> Only about three percent apart.

83
00:10:06,020 --> 00:10:18,010
But you can see it on the calendar. From the March equinox to the September one takes about 186 days.

84
00:10:18,090 --> 00:10:26,710
>> And the other half of the year takes only about 179, because Earth passes perihelion then, moving faster.

85
00:10:26,790 --> 00:10:31,450
>> So the two halves of the year aren't the same length.

86
00:10:31,530 --> 00:10:38,570
>> No. In the north, spring and summer together last about a week longer than autumn and winter.

87
00:10:38,650 --> 00:10:50,420
>> Mars swings more: about 26.5 km/s near the Sun, and about 22 at the far end.

88
00:10:50,500 --> 00:11:00,460
>> But comets show it best. Halley's Comet dips to about 0.6 AU from the Sun, then swings out to about 35.

89
00:11:00,540 --> 00:11:07,350
>> And one AU is Earth's average distance from the Sun.

90
00:11:07,430 --> 00:11:10,970
>> So at the far end, it's out beyond Neptune.

91
00:11:11,050 --> 00:11:22,610
>> And out there, in December 2023, it was crawling along at just 0.91 km/s.

92
00:11:22,690 --> 00:11:25,410
>> So how fast is it near the Sun?

93
00:11:25,490 --> 00:11:39,430
>> At the two ends, speed times distance is the same. So near the Sun, it races along at about 54 km/s.

94
00:11:39,510 --> 00:11:44,420
>> About fifty-nine times closer, so about fifty-nine times faster.

95
00:11:44,500 --> 00:11:59,520
>> Of its roughly 75-year orbit, it spends only about two years closer to the Sun than Jupiter. It's due back in 2061.

96
00:12:02,720 --> 00:12:08,490
>> Kepler wasn't done. He wanted a rule that linked all the planets, not just one.

97
00:12:08,570 --> 00:12:16,660
>> By his own account, the answer came to him on 8 March 1618.

98
00:12:16,740 --> 00:12:24,480
>> Square a planet's time to go round. Cube its distance from the Sun. The ratio is the same for every planet.

99
00:12:24,560 --> 00:12:26,750
>> Try it with Jupiter.

100
00:12:26,830 --> 00:12:34,270
>> Jupiter takes 11.86 years to go round. Squared, that's about 141.

101
00:12:34,350 --> 00:12:45,120
>> And it's 5.2 AU from the Sun. Cubed, that's about 141 again.

102
00:12:45,200 --> 00:12:51,140
>> With Kepler's own numbers, all six known planets agree to within about two percent.

103
00:12:51,220 --> 00:12:55,560
>> "I first believed I was dreaming," he wrote.

104
00:12:55,640 --> 00:12:59,330
>> After forty failed ovals, you can see why.

105
00:12:59,410 --> 00:13:07,280
>> This third law appeared in 1619, in his book The Harmony of the World.

106
00:13:07,360 --> 00:13:15,400
>> And there, Kepler heard music in the orbits. Earth's turning speed, seen from the Sun, varies by about a semitone.

107
00:13:15,480 --> 00:13:19,670
>> A semitone: the step from mi to fa.

108
00:13:19,750 --> 00:13:25,910
>> "The Earth sings Mi, Fa, Mi," he wrote: you may hear "misery" and "famine" in the syllables.

109
00:13:25,990 --> 00:13:28,980
>> A pun, four hundred years old.

110
00:13:29,060 --> 00:13:36,720
In 1621, his Epitome gave the area law its modern form.

111
00:13:36,800 --> 00:13:43,850
>> That same year, he also noted that the third law fits Jupiter's four bright moons.

112
00:13:46,450 --> 00:13:50,610
>> So why should planets obey these rules at all?

113
00:13:50,690 --> 00:13:53,350
>> That's where Isaac Newton comes in.

114
00:13:53,430 --> 00:14:01,420
In 1684 he proved that anything pulled toward a fixed centre sweeps out equal areas in equal times.

115
00:14:01,500 --> 00:14:04,470
>> Anything? Not just planets?

116
00:14:04,550 --> 00:14:13,660
>> Anything. His proof, in the Principia of 1687, is beautifully simple. Imagine the pull comes in short, sharp tugs.

117
00:14:13,740 --> 00:14:22,180
>> With no pull, the planet goes straight, covering equal distances in equal times.

118
00:14:22,260 --> 00:14:28,800
>> Each step makes a triangle with the Sun. Same base, same height: same area.

119
00:14:28,880 --> 00:14:37,800
>> Same base because the steps are equal, and same height because they're on the same straight line.

120
00:14:37,880 --> 00:14:44,020
>> Now a tug toward the Sun bends the path. But it pulls only along the line to the Sun.

121
00:14:44,100 --> 00:14:54,290
>> So the new triangle keeps the same base and the same height as before. Same area again.

122
00:14:54,370 --> 00:15:01,360
>> They share the base from the Sun to B, and the tug slides c parallel to it, so the height stays put.

123
00:15:01,440 --> 00:15:12,690
>> Make the tugs smaller and more frequent, and the path becomes a smooth orbit. The areas stay equal.

124
00:15:13,390 --> 00:15:19,880
>> Notice what the proof never needed: how strong the pull is. Any pull toward a centre will do.

125
00:15:19,960 --> 00:15:25,400
>> Even a pull that gets stronger with distance, like this one?

126
00:15:25,480 --> 00:15:30,550
>> Even that. Today we call this conservation of angular momentum.

127
00:15:30,630 --> 00:15:41,970
>> The ellipse and the third law need more: a pull that weakens with the square of the distance, as gravity does.

128
00:15:45,170 --> 00:15:54,010
>> Funny thing: Kepler never called these laws. The word first appeared in print in 1738, in a book by Voltaire.

129
00:15:54,090 --> 00:16:00,180
>> And the tidy set of three came later still, in 1804.

130
00:16:00,260 --> 00:16:05,370
>> So, one: a planet's orbit is an ellipse, with the Sun at one focus.

131
00:16:05,450 --> 00:16:12,290
>> Two: the line from the Sun sweeps equal areas in equal times.

132
00:16:12,370 --> 00:16:17,160
>> Three: the period squared goes with the distance cubed.

133
00:16:17,240 --> 00:16:22,380
>> All because Kepler refused to ignore eight arcminutes.

134
00:16:22,460 --> 00:16:24,830
>> A quarter of the Moon's width.

135
00:16:24,910 --> 00:16:37,580
>> So this January, when it's coldest in the north, remember: Earth is at its closest to the Sun, and moving fastest.
